BT (Introduction This research paper investigates cybersecurity governance and zero-trust authentication in Kenyan higher education portals. The purpose and framework of this paper focus on preventing unauthorized credential sharing.  Literature & Data Evidence Recent findings by Otieno [2024] indicate that multi-factor authentication reduces unauthorized portal access by 78%. Source data from the Kenya Cyber Security Report [2023] highlights the importance of role-based access control.  Analysis When compared to static passwords, zero-trust session tokens evaluate device posture continuously. However, usability limitations must be balanced for visually impaired faculty and students.  Conclusion & Summary In summary, zero-trust recommendations should pair strong security with accessible single-tap authentication. References: Otieno [2024].) Tj ET
